A||N||11||superXmotorsports||grinding 4 speed||||1||
Z||000000||superXmotorsports||04-13-2000||08:51 PM||||Hi everyone.  I encouraged a neighbor to buy a Fiero for his son.  Unfortunately he bought one from someone else.  It's a 86 GT 4spd.  The second day he had it the trans developed a major problem.  The transmission is grinding constantly even when in neutral.  You can here it when you engage and disengage the clutch too.  Any thoughts?<BR>Thanks!  SXM.||||reg||1
Z||000001||bHooper||04-13-2000||10:32 PM||||best case... the clutch isnt fully engageing, could be the pedal or the clutch itself.<P>worst case... bad synchros and would need a rebuild.<P>just a thought, as i am no expert, but have learned a lot in my explorations for a tranny for my car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||HF6582||04-14-2000||02:11 AM||||I would say bad input shaft bearing.<BR>Does it do it with and without the clucth pushed in?<P>Scott<p>[This message has been edited by HF6582 (edited 04-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||04-14-2000||02:25 AM||||<BR>As odd as it sounds, I think we determined in an earlier thread that the input shaft bearing is not even used. Therefore, there is no chance of it binding.<P>Is there a possibility that the shifter (cable) adjustment has simply gone out of whack? It would probably be easy enough to disconnect the cables at the transmission end to see if the tranny could be put in neutral (by hand) without any grinding.||||reg||1
Z||000004||HF6582||04-14-2000||04:43 AM||||The input shaft rides in bearings in the case.  With the age of these transmissions they will have wear on them. Never heard of a bearingless transmission before.  You may have taken it to mean that I was meaning the input bushing.  When that goes you usually have a chattering sound from the clutch.  If it makes the grinding noise without the clutch being pushed, I would say, the input shaft bearing in the case could possibbly be the cause.<BR>Syncros would only do it while trying to go gear to gear.  <BR>It could be possible, like Patrick stated, that the cables are out of addjustment, and holding the gears close enough to grind. I really don't think that is would be the problem, but it wouldn't hurt to check, though.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||04-14-2000||07:09 AM||||Went to the local Pontiac dealer looking for a pilot bearing - input shaft to flywheel bearing - and the v6 4sp doesn't have one but the 4cyl 5sp does||||reg||1
Z||000006||superXmotorsports||04-14-2000||07:40 AM||||It makes the sound when in gear and moving also...||||reg||1
Z||000007||fieroshop||04-14-2000||11:08 AM||||If the sound is constant you might have other bearings inside the transmission that are bad. One question that I will ask. Has anybody checked to see if the tranny has oil? That could also cause noise.||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||04-14-2000||11:41 AM||||oil would definately make a differance!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick||04-14-2000||01:54 PM||||<BR>Sorry for the confusion, but it was (too) late last night, and what I was thinking of was the <I>pilot bushing</I>.<P>According to information that we have discussed here before, the pilot bushing is only used with the automatic and not with any manual transmission in a Fiero. Read about it here:<P><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001081.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001081.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||mwbackus||04-14-2000||01:54 PM||||Would a bad throw out bearing do this?  I believe the input shaft bushing requires case separation, so the TO bearing may at least be worth a shot.  I am by no means a tranny expert, so consider this to be an uneducated hypothesis.<P>Mike B.<P><p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 04-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||HF6582||04-14-2000||02:05 PM||||Patrick, I didn't really make myself clear on what I was meaning in my first post, so I understand.<P>SuperX, the reason I say input shaft bearings, is that in your first post you stated that it does it while in netrual, that means clutch released only the input shaft is turning.<P>Scott||||reg||1
